  "how_to_read_it": "Two numbers decide it. The margin above the floor says how much room a delivered frame has before it trips; the margin below says how much room the control has before it escapes. If both are comfortable the floor's exact value is not what is doing the work, and a reviewer can propose another without changing a verdict. If either is thin, the value is load-bearing and was chosen after the fact, which would be a real objection.",
  "why_the_floor_is_not_being_moved": "The options table shows the data would carry a floor around 0.065 to 0.07: it balances the two margins and tightens the admissible shrink. It also shows 0.08 is unsafe, because its headroom falls below the movement the statistic already shows between reconstructions. But the movement estimate rests on four pairs, and the whole reason this report exists is that a threshold cannot be validated against the same nine frames it was chosen from. Tightening on that evidence would repeat the error with a smaller number, so the floor stays where it is and the asymmetry is recorded instead. A reviewer who wants 0.065 has the figures to ask for it.",
  "what_this_does_not_do": "It does not make the threshold pre-registered, and it does not show the floor catches every bad frame. It bounds one failure mode -- a subject that has shrunk away from the camera -- and says how much room there is on that axis. A frame can be badly framed at full size in ways no spread floor sees."
